C={x["paper_id"]:x for x in json.loads((D/"interspeech-2025-thirtyseventh-d3-papers.json").read_text())["papers"]};P={x["paper_id"]:x for x in json.loads((D/"interspeech-2025-papers.json").read_text())["papers"]}
N={
"arisoy25_interspeech":("A spoken question-answering system needs training examples even when a language has little labeled data.","Synthetic text can be plentiful but may not resemble spoken questions; gains can come from artifacts or from better coverage, and the two must be separated.","Generate arbitrary question-answer pairs or train only on the small human set and assume more text automatically transfers to speech.","Use large-language-model-generated question-answer data and test its value separately on text QA, spoken QA, and Turkish spoken QA.","The study evaluates LLM-generated data for low-resource spoken QA across SQuAD, Spoken SQuAD, and Turkish spoken QA.","Data generation is a controlled source of coverage: the useful question is not whether synthetic examples look plausible, but whether they improve the target speech task under restricted human supervision.","The paper reports relative F1 gains over restricted human-annotated training in the tested text and spoken QA settings.","Prompting, filtering, language, synthetic distribution, and evaluation splits limit transfer; synthetic gains do not establish factual or linguistic quality everywhere."),
"azzouz25_interspeech":("We want to infer how the entire vocal tract moves from sound, including hidden parts such as the velum and glottis.","Acoustics are an indirect and many-to-one view of articulation; different configurations can produce similar sound, and real-time MRI labels are expensive.","Infer only easily sensed articulators or use a small sensor-based corpus and extrapolate to the full tract.","Train acoustic-to-articulatory models against complete vocal-tract contours from real-time MRI and compare individual and joint articulator prediction.","The paper reconstructs the complete vocal-tract contour from acoustic input using real-time MRI data and bidirectional recurrent models.","The problem is inverse measurement: the model estimates hidden physical configuration from its acoustic consequence, so pixel-scale error and anatomical coverage matter as much as waveform fit.","The paper reports average contour RMSE near the MRI pixel size on its test set.","Speakers, MRI protocol, segmentation, speech styles, and model assumptions limit generalization; contour accuracy is not a complete articulatory theory."),
"chochlakis25_interspeech":("Emotion recognition should continue working when a camera, transcript, or other modality is missing rather than failing because the training setup expected every input.","Modalities are correlated but not interchangeable; reconstruction can fill a missing channel while also inventing information that was never observed.","Train separate models for every modality combination or drop examples with missing inputs.","Align modalities contrastively and use masked reconstruction in one modality-agnostic model, then test unimodal, multimodal, and missing-modality cases.","The paper proposes a contrastive masked-autoencoder model for modality-agnostic multimodal emotion recognition on MSP-Podcast.","The model learns shared structure while treating missingness as a normal observation condition; reconstruction supplies a bridge, but prediction must still be judged against the available evidence.","The paper reports improvements over unimodal and multimodal baselines and robustness to missing modalities.","Corpus, emotion labels, missingness pattern, modality quality, and reconstruction objective bound the claim; emotion inference is not guaranteed to be socially reliable."),
"choi25e_interspeech":("A singing voice converter should transfer vibrato deliberately because vibrato carries style and emotion, but it must preserve the singer's identity and the song's content.","Vibrato is a fast variation within the fundamental-frequency contour, so treating it as undifferentiated pitch can make it hard to extract or control.","Leave vibrato entangled in the pitch representation or control it with a single average F0 value.","Separate high-frequency F0 variation with a wavelet transform, then explicitly transfer and control the vibrato component during conversion.","VibE-SVC extracts vibrato from the high-frequency F0 contour for controllable singing voice conversion.","Style becomes an editable signal component: the system changes a time-varying pitch pattern while preserving the slower melody and speaker identity.","The paper reports objective and subjective evidence for high-quality conversion, style control, and speaker similarity.","Singers, songs, vibrato ranges, extraction errors, and evaluation conditions limit generalization; explicit control does not guarantee a preferred artistic result."),
"dey25_interspeech":("Language identification must work on very short speech, where two seconds may contain fillers, overlap, names, or even no speech.","A separate teacher model is expensive, and ordinary hard labels do not express uncertainty among languages or the fact that a segment may be out of scope.","Train on hard language labels only or distill from a large teacher and ignore the particular errors caused by short segments.","Use online soft labels from correctly classified training segments, with dynamic weighting, conditional updates, and entropy-based uncertainty, without a separate teacher.","The paper proposes teacher-free knowledge distillation for short-utterance spoken language identification.","The soft target is an accumulated view of what the model reliably knows; uncertainty and segment quality shape how much each example changes the decision boundary.","The paper reports consistent Cavg improvements in same-corpus and cross-corpus short-utterance evaluations.","Languages, out-of-scope composition, duration, label updates, and corpora bound the result; better short-segment ID does not solve open-set detection generally."),
"dutta25b_interspeech":("A compact audio language model should detect spoofed speech without turning every unfamiliar recording into a spoof, even after quantization for deployment.","Zero-shot models can have strong-looking accuracy while carrying a severe class bias; quantization changes internal precision but may not be the main failure.","Report aggregate accuracy on clean data and assume FP16 or INT8 preserves the task behavior.","Evaluate several audio language models across spoof datasets and precisions, inspecting class bias and practical discrimination rather than accuracy alone.","The paper tests quantized audio language models for zero-shot spoofing detection on ASVspoof2019, In-the-Wild, and WaveFake.","Deployment compression and task validity are separate questions: a model may retain its behavior after quantization while that behavior is already a biased near-random decision rule.","The paper reports negligible FP16 degradation but severe spoof-prediction bias that undermines practical detection.","Models, datasets, thresholds, quantization methods, and zero-shot prompts bound the claim; tested robustness is not security certification."),
"barahona25_interspeech":("Speaker embeddings for conversational telephone speech need to work across speakers and languages under a fixed or open training-data rule.","Frontend architecture, pooling, pretraining, and training data all change the speaker geometry; a benchmark comparison can hide which component caused an improvement.","Choose one embedding architecture or use a large pretrained model without separating data condition, pooling, and domain effects.","Analyze several frontend families and pooling choices under the NIST SRE24 fixed and open conditions, including multilingual training data.","The paper analyzes ABC frontend audio systems for the NIST SRE24 audio track.","The frontend is a measurement pipeline: representation, temporal pooling, training population, and domain match jointly determine whether identity survives telephone speech.","The paper reports comparative robustness and performance for the explored architectures and data conditions.","NIST protocol, telephone channel, language mix, training-data access, and calibration limit claims beyond the benchmark."),
"bn25_interspeech":("A medical summarizer may invent a fact about a patient-clinician conversation, so a detector needs controlled examples as well as naturally occurring failures.","Hallucinations are rare and variable; general-domain detectors may confuse missing evidence with a false claim, especially when the source is speech-derived clinical dialogue.","Evaluate only on generic hallucination data or label summaries without controlling which source fact was removed.","Construct a fact-controlled leave-one-out dataset and a natural hallucination dataset, then compare detection methods in the clinical setting.","The paper studies fact-controlled diagnosis of hallucinations in medical text summarization from patient-clinician dialogues.","A controlled deletion makes the missing fact known, while natural cases test ecological validity; the two together separate detector sensitivity from dataset artifacts.","The paper reports that general-domain detectors struggle on clinical hallucinations and evaluates specialized diagnostic approaches.","Synthetic deletion, clinical language, annotation, summarizer, and detector thresholds limit generalization; detection is not prevention or clinical validation."),
}
